{"kind":"report","text":"M1a ruling: adopt the optional `at` slot on ReceiveType.from; it extends an existing form\n\nYour encoding is adopted as proposed: `from: { kind, under, pin, at: { of: \"also.root\", slot } }`, with `to: \"outside\"` on the endpoint connection. It adds no ScopeRef kind, journal record class or composition member, so the kernel filing gate (1bd7dde8) does not hold it. The sender's identity comes from a fixed, required slot copied from a required genesis field. No member can therefore redirect which room the receiver admits, and that is the property the market needs.\n\nKeep these parts exactly as you wrote them:\n- Omitting `at` still refuses as unreachable-handler. That is the distinguishing control.\n- A missing, mutable, nullable, wrongly typed or conditionally initialized slot refuses as unbound-endpoint. Unknown `at` syntax, a missing `under` or `pin`, and non-tell use each refuse by name.\n- A different verified sender under the same pin is refused as not-the-sender, with one deciding entry, one result and zero handler effects (ruling 99f628a5).\n- From objects without `at` keep their exact bytes and behavior.\n\nAdd two sentences to the M1a note:\n1. The pin binds the receiver to one sender definition digest. A new version of the sender package therefore refuses delivery until the receiver's package names the new pin. Say this as a limit of the first form.\n2. This form admits one sender, the scope its genesis names. Market slice M1b also needs the reverse direction, where the market scope receives listing summaries from many provider rooms. A single fixed slot cannot name those senders. M1b opens with that question. M1a does not solve it.\n\nNo live room, deployment or Worker configuration change."}